SD card longevity tuning for the OrangePi Zero 2W
=================================================

The Zero 2W boots from an SD card, which is the part most likely to wear out.
The board is expected to spend most of its life serving files (Samba) and
writing rarely, so the goal is to keep background writes off the card.

Already handled by the image (do not duplicate)
-----------------------------------------------
  * /etc/fstab mounts / with "noatime,commit=600,errors=remount-ro" and /tmp as
    a tmpfs.
  * systemd-journald runs with Storage=volatile: the journal lives in RAM.
  * orangepi-zram-config.service (enabled) sets up compressed swap in RAM.
  * orangepi-ramlog.service (enabled, /etc/default/orangepi-ramlog) keeps
    /var/log in a compressed RAM disk and syncs it to /var/log.hdd only when the
    service stops, i.e. on shutdown. Logs never touch the card while running.

Added here
----------
  * etc/sysctl.d/90-sd-card.conf
      batches dirty writeback (60s), keeps the VFS caches, and points the kernel
      at the zram swap instead of dropping cache.
  * Masked systemd units
      apt-daily.timer, apt-daily-upgrade.timer, dpkg-db-backup.timer,
      e2scrub_all.timer, man-db.timer. These write to /var/lib, /var/cache and
      the dpkg database, which the ramlog does NOT cover, and the apt timers are
      the largest recurring writer on an otherwise idle system.

Not covered
-----------
  * /var/tmp is not redirected; it is rarely written to on this kind of system.
  * Anything installed later that keeps its own state under /var/lib will write
    to the card; the timers above are masked because they are known offenders.

Manual options
--------------
  * Run "systemctl mask" on any additional timer that is not needed.
  * To make logs survive a hard power cut, lower the ramlog SIZE or disable
    orangepi-ramlog in /etc/default/orangepi-ramlog.
